            "body": "Checked this out locally. The three new test functions (five cases with the parametrization) pass on this branch and the first one fails on main with `user_count_estimate` equal to the request rate instead of 5, so the regression is real. I ran them with the repo's heavy imports stubbed since I don't have a GPU/torch environment here, so treat it as a check of `_prepare_records` alone.\n\nTwo notes, neither blocking.\n\n1. The bug needs the user-count key to be absent. `bench serve` always writes `max_concurrency` into the result JSON (it's `None` when the flag isn't set), and `_first_present` returns the first key that exists even when its value is `None`, so `_get_numeric` returns `None` and the old code already fell through to `max_concurrent_requests`. I confirmed that with a record shaped like real `bench serve` output. The `request_rate` fallback only bites when the key named by `--user-count-var` is missing from the record, for example a custom `--user-count-var` that a sweep didn't record. Might be worth saying that in the description so nobody expects a change for ordinary runs. A test with `max_concurrency: None` present would pin the common shape too.\n\n2. After this, the fallback is the measured `max_concurrent_requests`, and that number is currently overcounted at integer-second boundaries (#56653, with #56647, #56675 and #56678 open for it). Until one of those lands, the tokens/s/user for runs that hit the fallback will read low, by up to 2x in the numbers reported in #56653. It's the right fallback, just worth linking in the description.",
